Port patch 0038 - audio: the sample rings the RSP reads (announcer / cry stream, Game Boy sound) are copied when the command is built
Why: n_alMainBusPull (src/libnaudio/n_mainbus.c) builds two aLoadBuffer commands per pull that make the RSP read 0x170 bytes of a ring the CPU keeps filling
(the streamed voice rings D_800FCF28[0..1], which carry the announcer's speech and the Pokemon cries, and the Game Boy sound ring D_800FC6D8). The producer
(func_80044EA4 in src/audio_stream.c) keeps its write position well ahead of the read position D_800FCF30 that this function advances, and it is
calibrated for a console where the RSP runs the command list right after it is built (the read position is 1 to 3 pulls ahead of the load at most).
In the port the audio microcode runs on its own thread (and gets delayed behind other work), and the load happened up to 6 pulls after the pointer moved: measured on
the Linux build in a battle (KYUJO_STREAM_DUMP / an RSP DMA probe), 63 percent of the loads were 4 to 6 pulls late and 3.5 percent of all loads straddled
the producer's write position or had been lapped (the region had already been overwritten with newer samples): the announcer's speech got clicks and static,
on Windows, Linux and the Switch alike (the Stadium recompilation project lists the same "announcer speech still carries static during battles"). With the
audio task run synchronously the lag is 1 to 3 pulls and torn loads fall to 0.2 percent, which confirms the cause.
The patch copies the 184 samples of each pull into a slot of a 16-slot ring of snapshots at build time and points the commands at the copy, so the RSP reads
exactly what the console would have read. The producer and the rest of the audio are unchanged. Slots are not reused before the task has run (lag measured: up to 6 pulls; 16 slots per source leave a margin of more than two; 17 KB of image).
Files: `src/libnaudio/n_mainbus.c`

--- a/src/libnaudio/n_mainbus.c
+++ b/src/libnaudio/n_mainbus.c
@@ -27,9 sha256=bc89c52b1d17db78b1fcc2a9e2d54a03d8c8a309173e9c2930ed2315a1d8caa3 @@
=3
+/* port patch 0038: the RSP reads the CPU-written sample rings (the announcer / cry stream and the Game Boy sound) when the audio task runs, which on the
+ * console is right after the command list is built, but in the port can be several pulls later; by then the producer has written further and lapped the
+ * region, and the load returned newer samples than the pull was built for (a click or static). Each pull therefore copies its 0x170 bytes from the ring
+ * into a slot of its own at BUILD time and the command refers to the copy. 16 slots per source are not reused before the task has run (the port's lag
+ * was measured at up to 6 pulls, so 16 leaves a margin of more than two). */
+#define PULL_SNAP_SLOTS 16
+#define PULL_SNAP_SAMPLES 0xB8
+static s16 sPullSnap[3][PULL_SNAP_SLOTS][PULL_SNAP_SAMPLES] __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+static u32 sPullSnapNext[3];
+
+static s16* PullSnapshot(s32 source, const s16* src) {
+    s16* dst = sPullSnap[source][sPullSnapNext[source]];
+    s32 k;
+
+    sPullSnapNext[source] = (sPullSnapNext[source] + 1) % PULL_SNAP_SLOTS;
+    for (k = 0; k < PULL_SNAP_SAMPLES; k++) {
+        dst[k] = src[k];
+    }
+    return dst;
+}
+
=3
+    s16* snap;
=3
